<wp:post_password></wp:post_password>
<wp:is_sticky>0</wp:is_sticky>
</item>
<item>
<title>Using groups.io</title>
<link>https://www.theapplepi.org/using-groupsio/</link>
<pubDate>Tue, 22 Sep 2026 12:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
<dc:creator><![CDATA[pi-editor]]></dc:creator>
<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.theapplepi.org/using-groupsio.html</guid>
<description></description>
<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:image {"sizeSlug":"large","linkDestination":"custom"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image alignright size-large"><a href="https://applepi.groups.io/g/main"><img src="https://www.theapplepi.org/uploads/1/4/5/6/145684944/editor/groupsio-icon_2.png" alt="Link to Groups IO"/></a></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"align":"right"} -->
<p class="has-text-align-right"><strong><span style="color:#5040ae">Link to Groups.io</span></strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"textAlign":"center","style":{"color":{"text":"#5040ae"}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color has-text-align-center" style="color:#5040ae"><span style="color:#5040ae;font-size:2.4rem">Using groups.io</span></h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:columns -->
<div class="wp-block-columns">
<!-- wp:column {"width":"57.692307692308%"} -->
<div class="wp-block-column" style="flex-basis:57.692307692308%"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong><span style="color:#5040ae"><span style="font-size:1.35rem">groups.io ...</span></span></strong><br>​... is used by Pi members to share information, help each other, and request assistance. groups.io is an online service that supports both interactive email and posting to the Pi&#x27;s groups.io pages. Between meetings, groups.io is where all the Pi action happens, and it is your direct line for technical assistance. <strong><span style="color:#5040ae"><a href="/why-join-groupsio/">Click here</a></span></strong> to learn more about what groups.io has to offer Pi members.<br><br>This page will show you how to:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Create a groups.io account</li><li>Log into your groups.io account</li><li>Request access to the Pi pages on groups.io</li><li>Access and navigate the Pi pages on groups.​​</li><li>Search groups.io for topics of interest</li><li>​Start a new groups.io Special Interest Group</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list --></div>
<!-- /wp:column -->

<!-- wp:column {"width":"42.307692307692%"} -->
<div class="wp-block-column" style="flex-basis:42.307692307692%"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><em><span style="color:#5040ae"><strong>Using this information as a How To Guide:</strong></span><br>​Keep this page open in one tab (or window) of your browser and open groups.io in another tab. You can then easily switch back and forth from the steps outlined below to the groups.io pages where you can see what we are talking about or follow along to accomplish what you want to do.</em></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:column -->
</div>
<!-- /wp:columns -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong><span style="color:#5040ae;font-size:1.35rem">Create a groups.io account:</span></strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list {"ordered":true} -->
<ol class="wp-block-list"><li>If you already have a groups.io account for one or more other organizations, login to your account and request access to the pi pages (see the two procedures below). Otherwise,...</li><li>If you don&#x27;t have a groups.io account, click to go to <strong><span style="color:#5040ae"><a href="http://applepi.groups.io/register" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">applepi.groups.io/register</a>.</span></strong></li><li>Enter the email address to use for your groups.io account. This email can be different than the email you used for your Pi Join It account, but we strongly suggest you use the same email for both to avoid future confusion.</li><li>Enter the password you want to use for the groups.io account you are creating.</li><li>Click <strong>CREATE AN ACCOUNT</strong>.​</li></ol>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong><span style="color:#5040ae;font-size:1.35rem">Log into your groups.io account:</span></strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list {"ordered":true} -->
<ol class="wp-block-list"><li>Click to go to <strong><span style="color:#5040ae"><a href="http://groups.io/login" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">groups.io/login</a>.</span></strong></li><li>Enter your groups.io email address and password.</li><li>Click <strong>LOG IN</strong>.​</li></ol>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong><span style="color:#5040ae;font-size:1.35rem">Request access to the Pi pages on groups.io:</span></strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list {"ordered":true} -->
<ol class="wp-block-list"><li>Click to go to <strong><span style="color:#5040ae"><a href="http://applepi.groups.io/g/main" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">applepi.groups.io/g/main</a>.</span></strong></li><li>Click <strong>APPLY FOR MEMBERSHIP IN THIS GROUP</strong>. Note: you only need to do this once. After your request is approved, you will receive an email notification to your Pi account email address (the one you provided to Join It). This approval may take a day or two.</li><li>You will now be able to access the Pi pages in groups.io. See the next procedure.​</li></ol>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong><span style="color:#5040ae;font-size:1.35rem">Access and navigate the Pi pages on groups.io:</span></strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list {"ordered":true} -->
<ol class="wp-block-list"><li>Click to go to <strong><span style="color:#5040ae"><a href="http://applepi.groups.io/g/main" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">applepi.groups.io/g/main</a>.</span></strong></li><li>If your name appears in the top right of the page, go to step 4.</li><li>If your name does not appear, click <strong>LOG IN</strong> to log in with your groups.io email and password. A locally stored cookie should keep you logged into the site unless you specifically log out.</li><li>This is the main Pi page in groups.io. You can return to this page my clicking <strong>HOME</strong>. The primary locations on this page are described below. Click on each section to go there. Clicking HOME will return you to the main Pi page.<!-- wp:list {"ordered":true} -->
<ol class="wp-block-list"><li><strong>SUBSCRIPTION</strong>: The Subscription area is where you can set your preferences for how you are notified about messages that are posted. Various options are available for importance and frequency.</li><li><strong>MESSAGES</strong>: Here you can view the topics (information, questions, answers) posted by Pi members. You can view by Topic, which keeps all messages for that topic together, or you can view by message which presents all posted messages in chronological order. If you have selected a subgroup (see below), only the messages for that subgroup will be displayed.</li><li><strong>NEW TOPIC</strong>: Click here to post a new discussion topic or to ask a question of Pi members. If you have selected a subgroup (see below), your new post will only appear in the messages for that subgroup.</li><li><strong>SUBGROUPS</strong>: The Pi pages in groups.io include several Subgroups which you may optionally join. Click here to learn about them and request to join the ones that interest you.</li><li><strong>YOUR GROUPS</strong> (in the top banner): Click here to see all of the your groups.io affiliations, including the Pi. Any Pi subgroups you have joined will also be shown. Select Washington Apple Pi to go to the main Pi page. Select a particular Pi subgroup to switch from the main Pi area to the groups.io area for that subgroup.</li><li><strong>CALENDAR</strong>: Click this section to display a calendar of Pi events or, if you have switched to a subgroup, the events for that subgroup.</li><li><strong>DIRECTORY</strong>:Click this section to display a directory of the Pi members. If the Directory doesn&#x27;t display, you will receive a message that you need to change your Profile Privacy to &quot;Other Members of Your Group.&quot; The message will contain a link to allow you to easily do this. We encourage all Pi members to use this setting. It will allow you to, right from the Directory, email other Pi members and others to email you.</li></ol>
<!-- /wp:list --></li></ol>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong><span style="color:#5040ae;font-size:1.35rem">Search groups.io for topics of interest:</span></strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list {"ordered":true} -->
<ol class="wp-block-list"><li>Click to go to <strong><span style="color:#5040ae"><a href="https://applepi.groups.io/g/main/topics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">applepi.groups.io/g/main/topics</a>. </span></strong>This is the Messages page of the main Apple Pi area.</li><li>Type the topic you are searching for in the Search field at the upper right of the page. Enclose search terms of more than one word in quotation marks. For example, you might search for M2, &quot;iPhone 16&quot;, &quot;chat notes&quot;, &quot;stage manager&quot;, or HomePod.</li><li>Hit Return or click the magnifying glass icon next to the Search field.​</li><li>This will search the main Pi area. To search only within the postings for a subgroup, switch to that subgroup (see above Your Groups procedure) and then submit your search.</li></ol>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong><span style="color:#5040ae;font-size:1.35rem">Start a new groups.io Special Interest Group:</span></strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list {"ordered":true} -->
<ol class="wp-block-list"><li>Send an email to the <strong><span style="color:#5040ae"><a href="mailto:president@wap.org">Pi President</a> </span></strong>proposing your idea for a new Special Interest Group (SIG).</li><li>Optionally, for priority consideration, authorize an electronic transfer of 100 Bitcoin to the Pi.</li><li>Be alert, this request could very well be interpreted as your volunteering to be the discussion leader for the SIG.</li></ol>
<!-- /wp:list -->]]></content:encoded>
<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[]]></excerpt:encoded>
<wp:post_id>156</wp:post_id>
<wp:post_date>2026-09-22 12:00:00</wp:post_date>
<wp:post_date_gmt>2026-09-22 16:00:00</wp:post_date_gmt>
<wp:post_modified>2026-09-22 12:00:00</wp:post_modified>
<wp:post_modified_gmt>2026-09-22 16:00:00</wp:post_modified_gmt>
<wp:comment_status>closed</wp:comment_status>
<wp:ping_status>closed</wp:ping_status>
<wp:post_name>using-groupsio</wp:post_name>
<wp:status>publish</wp:status>
<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
<wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
<wp:post_type>page</wp:post_type>
<wp:post_password></wp:post_password>
<wp:is_sticky>0</wp:is_sticky>
</item>
<item>
<title>Outreach/Partners</title>
<link>https://www.theapplepi.org/outreachpartners/</link>
<pubDate>Tue, 22 Sep 2026 12:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
<dc:creator><![CDATA[pi-editor]]></dc:creator>
<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.theapplepi.org/outreachpartners.html</guid>
<description></description>
<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"textAlign":"center"} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-align-center">Outreach/Partners</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:columns -->
<div class="wp-block-columns">
<!-- wp:column {"width":"50%"} -->
<div class="wp-block-column" style="flex-basis:50%"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The Pi has associations and connections with a number of other organizations. Though we are not specifically endorsing these organizations over any others, those listed have provided Pi members with positive experiences and satisfying relationships over the years.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><li><strong>George Mason University Pi Scholarship:</strong> The Pi has sponsored a scholarship at the GMU College of Engineering and Computing since 2017. The scholarship provides financial assistance to students studying information technology at the College. Use the link below to contribute to the GMU Pi Scholarship endowment. Your gift will help ensure that the Pi endowment can become and continue to remain self-sustaining.  Contribute to the scholarship <strong><a href="https://advancement.gmu.edu/24CECPI" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">here</a>.</strong><strong> </strong></li><li><strong>Leisure World:</strong>  Leisure World of Maryland is 55 and older community in Montgomery County, Maryland. Some Pi members reside here, and the Pi has had to opportunity to conduct technical support sessions for residents.</li><li><strong>Pozez JCC:</strong> T<span style="color:#000000">he Pozez JCC is committed to building Jewish Connection, Community, and Peoplehood throughout Northern Virginia. The JCC has graciously hosted in-person Pi meetings and, in return, Pi members have provided hardware and software assistance to JCC members attending our sessions.</span></li><li><strong>Take Control Books:</strong> Many Pi members always want to &quot;know more,&quot; and are therefore fans of the Take Control books available <strong><a href="https://www.takecontrolbooks.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">here</a></strong>. The books are well written and allow for a deeper dive into many Apple-related subjects. Take Control authors have given well-received presentations to the Pi.</li><li><strong>MacMost:</strong> This is a site for a steady stream of Mac Video Tutorials and Tips for Apple Users. Even the most knowledgeable user can discover something new in each video release. Not surprisingly, MacMost content can be found at <strong><a href="http://macmost.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">macmost.com</a></strong>.</li><li><strong>The Bearded Teacher:</strong> Interested in the latest Apple hardware and software reviews as well as a wealth of Shortcuts and Home Automation material? This is a good place to visit for fast-paced education. Check out the Bearded Teacher <strong><a href="https://beard.fm/bio" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">here</a></strong>.</li><li><strong>London Mac Users Group: </strong>This is our &quot;across the pond&quot; connection. Some Pi members are also members of this UK-based users&#x27; group, and one of their members is on the Pi Board of Directors. You might drop in on this colourful organisation <strong><a href="https://www.lmug.org.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">here</a></strong>.</li><li><strong>Mac Recycle Clinic:</strong> The Pi is a big supporter of and has had a long-standing relationship with the Mac Recycle Clinic. The clinic accepts and refurbishes Intel, Mac, and Chromebook computers and, where possible, returns the computers for continued use in the community. Learn more about their important work <strong><a href="https://www.macrecycleclinic.org/site/about-2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">here</a></strong><strong><span style="color:#5040ae">.</span></strong>​</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list --></div>
<!-- /wp:column -->

<!-- wp:column {"width":"50%"} -->
<div class="wp-block-column" style="flex-basis:50%"><!-- wp:image {"sizeSlug":"large","linkDestination":"custom"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large"><img src="https://www.theapplepi.org/uploads/1/4/5/6/145684944/hands-1939895-1280_orig.png" alt="Picture"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image --></div>
<!-- /wp:column -->
</div>
<!-- /wp:columns -->]]></content:encoded>
